I challenge Tim or anyone to provide a representation of what their senses
are "perceiving" BEFORE such "perceptions" have been interpreted by their
consciousness.  Tim and Rick Pasotto are redefining "perception" to exclude
the act of interpretation which is implicit in the conventional definition
of the word.  (Dictionaries provide two kinds of definitions for
"perception": the common usage is defined as synonymous with "cognition",
while the technical definition in the field of psychology explicitly
identifies "perception" as "an awareness" of a stimulus.)

Let's try a "reverse brain-in-the-vat".  (Sounds like a horrible idea for a
California fast food chain.  "Welcome to Brain-In-The-Vat; may I take your
order?")  We insert tiny monitors in each of the receptor cells ("rods and
cones") of the retina of Tim's eye.  Each of these monitors records exactly
the time and intensity of neural firings in these cells and these data are
recorded on a big hard disk somewhere.

If the data are merely stored, not interpreted, no _perception_ is taking
place, according to the accepted definition of the word.  The data may
accurately reflect the history of light to which the eye was exposed, but
_perception_ has not happened yet.

>the failure to identify an assassin camouflaged in bushes you walk past
>who then jumps and kills you is not a perceptual failure, but a failure of
>perceptul judgement.  You perceived the assassin; you just didn't judge his
>identity correctly.

"Perceptual judgement" is "perception"; there is no "perception"
independent of the perceiver's evaluation of incoming sense data.  Suppose
that Tim were attacked in the manner described after we have
short-circuited his retinas and piped all of their visual impressions to
computer storage instead of to his brain.  By the Objectivist definition of
"perception", Tim "perceived" the attacker, but by any conventional
definition of the word, the notion that Tim perceived his attacker is
absurd.
