If I were given two minutes to come up with lists, I'd say:

EXTROPIAN VIRTUES:  Selfishness, rationality, greed, hubris, love,
tolerance, and good hygiene.

EXTROPIAN VICES:  Humility, innumeracy, susceptibility to peer pressure,
envy, puritanism, faith, and certainty.

Classifying "greed" as a virtue but "envy" as a vice might seem confusing;
but Tom Morrow assures me that the distinction between "envy" and similar
mental states like "jealousy" and "covetousness" is that the envious person
not only wants to have what the other person has, but to deprive them of it
as well.  The merely greedy (or jealous or covetous) are content to let the
other person keep what they have, just so long as they themselves can have
it, too.  Envy is inherently zero-sum, while greed et al can be
positive-sum.

We need a shorter term for "susceptibility to peer pressure", BTW.
